\nL324: ## Appendix A Proofs of Theoretical Results\nL325: \nL326: ### A.1 Proof of cite66†Lemma 3.1 L327: ###### Proof.\nL328: \nL329: For any open set $U\\subset\\mathbb{R}^{d}$, by the definition of probability flow, we have\nL330: \nL331:  | $$\\int_{\\phi_{t}(U)}p_{t}(x)\\ dx=\\int_{U}p_{0}(x)\\ dx.$$  |  | (12)\nL332: \nL333: Since $\\phi_{t}:\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ defines a homeomorphism, we apply the change of variable formula for the right hand side:\nL334: \nL335:  | $$\\int_{U}p_{0}(x)\\ dx=\\int_{\\phi_{t}(U)}p_{0}(\\phi_{t}^{-1}(x))|\\det(\\nabla\\phi_{t}^{-1}(x))|\\ dx,$$  |  | (13)\nL336: \nL337: thus,\nL338:  | $$\\int_{\\phi_{t}(U)}p_{t}(x)\\ dx=\\int_{\\phi_{t}(U)}p_{0}(\\phi_{t}^{-1}(x))|\\det(\\nabla\\phi_{t}^{-1}(x))|\\ dx.$$  |  | (14)\nL339: \nL340: Because $U$ can be arbitrary, the above immediately implies\nL341: \nL342:  | $$p_{t}(x)=p_{0}(\\phi_{t}^{-1}(x))|\\det(\\nabla\\phi_{t}^{-1}(x))|.$$  |  | (15)\nL343: \nL344: ∎\nL345: ### A.2 Proof of cite112†Theorem 3.3 L346: ###### Proof.\nL347: \nL348: Since $\\phi_{T}:\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R^{d}}$ is a homeomorphism, from the definition of data class and latent class, it is straightforward to see $Z=\\cup_{c\\in\\mathcal{C}}Z_{c}$, with each $Z_{c}$ connected and $Z_{c}$’s mutually disjoint.\nL349: \nL350: By cite66†Lemma 3.1 and the definition of probability flow, we have\nL351:  | $$\\begin{split}\\mathcal{Z}(x|Z_{c})=&\\frac{\\mathcal{Z}(x)}{\\int_{Z_{c}}\\mathcal{Z}(x)\\ dx}\\\\\nL352: =&\\frac{|\\det(\\nabla\\phi_{T}^{-1}(x))|\\mathcal{D}({\\phi}_{T}^{-1}(x))}{\\int_{D_{c}=\\phi_{T}^{-1}(Z_{c})}\\mathcal{D}(y)\\ dy}\\\\\nL353: =&|\\det(\\nabla\\phi_{T}^{-1}(x))|\\mathcal{D}_{c}({\\phi}_{T}^{-1}(x))=\\mathcal{Z}_{c}(x).\\end{split}$$  |  | (16)\nL354: \nL355: ∎\nL356: ## Appendix B More Details on Experimental Results\nL357: \nL358: This appendix provides supplementary details for cite13†Section 5 .\nL359: ### B.1 Cross-Level Model Accuracy Heatmap\nL360: \nL361: We report the full cross-level accuracy heatmap for cite77†Figure 2(a) with per-cell numeric values (See cite113†Figure 6(a) ). We also include the corresponding heatmap obtained by replacing the MLP with an LDA classifier, which turns out almost identical, suggesting that the observed predictability is intrinsic rather than an artifact of the classifier choice (See cite114†Figure 6(b) ).\nL362: \nL363: cite115†Image: Refer to caption (a) Neural network model\nL364: cite116†Image: Refer to caption (b) LDA model\nL365: \nL366: Figure 6: Cross-level classification accuracy heatmaps for diffusion noise across confidence levels (with per-cell accuracies) through the (a) neural network model and (b) LDA model.\nL367: ### B.2 LDA–UMAP Embedding\nL368: \nL369: We include the full LDA–UMAP embedding of latent seeds across all confidence levels for cite81†Figure 3(a) , which reveals a smooth transition in latent geometry from ordered structure at high confidence to increasingly chaotic organization at low confidence (See cite117†Figure 7 ).\nL370: \nL371: cite118†Image: Refer to caption Figure 7: LDA–UMAP embeddings of latent seeds across all confidence levels.\nL372: ## Appendix C Latent Structure Disappearance with Stochastic Models\nL373: A deterministic sampler is essential for latent classification, since it induces a well-defined map from a seed to a generated sample (and hence to a label), making it meaningful to discuss latent structure. To validate this point, we repeat the experiments in cite13†Section 5 after replacing DDIM with DDPM sampling.\nL374: As anticipated, stochasticity breaks the seed-to-class correspondence: both the cross-level label predictability (cite78†Figure 2(b) , or cite119†Figure 8 for details) and the LDA–UMAP embeddings (cite120†Figure 9 ) collapse, showing neither stable transfer across confidence levels, nor coherent class separation. In short, under stochastic sampling, the latent structure becomes effectively unlearnable.\nL375: cite121†Image: Refer to caption (a) Neural network model\nL376: \nL377: cite122†Image: Refer to caption (b) LDA model\nL378: Figure 8: Cross-level classification accuracy heatmaps for diffusion noise across confidence levels (with per-cell accuracies) under DDPM setting through the (a) neural network model and (b) LDA model. Both models exhibit a heterogeneous accuracy across levels with no recognizable structure. cite123†Image: Refer to caption Figure 9: Separability in DDPM noises fails to appear under LDA-UMAP and UMAP only embedding.\nL379: ## Appendix D LDA-UMAP Pipeline on Denoised Images\nL380: \nL381: We apply the LDA–UMAP pipeline introduced in cite13†Section 5 to images generated by the DDIM diffusion model. For each confidence level, we sample 3,500 denoised images, with 350 images per class label, yielding a balanced dataset across levels and classes.\nL382: As expected, denoised images exhibit strong intrinsic class-aligned clustering, with clustering strength decreasing as confidence levels drop. In this case, LDA–UMAP pipeline reveals class-aligned organization more clearly at lower confidence levels than UMAP alone, although both methods indicate degraded structure relative to high-confidence samples.\nL383: \nL384: cite124†Image: Refer to caption L385: \nL386: cite125†Image: Refer to caption "
